WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A park planner needs a model kiosk, a delivery route and a
reflected sign.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.160 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each drawing looks attractive, but can the pieces work
together?

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:08.680 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will combine our spatial reasoning skills.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:17.166 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check each decision with evidence and keep track of
the order in which changes happen.

00:00:17.166 --> 00:00:17.566 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to combine checked nets, routes and
transformations in one design.

00:00:17.566 --> 00:00:29.846 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:29.846 --> 00:00:31.246 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:31.246 --> 00:00:36.630 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:41.750 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to combine checked nets, routes and
transformations in one design.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.590 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:52.012 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:52.012 --> 00:00:54.652 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:54.652 --> 00:00:56.972 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can justify a valid net.

00:00:56.972 --> 00:00:59.212 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can communicate a checked route.

00:00:59.212 --> 00:01:02.252 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can verify transformations and properties.

00:01:02.252 --> 00:01:06.132 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:14.860 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.660 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use card for the kiosk, squared paper for the map and
tracing paper for the sign.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.620 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each material helps answer a different question.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.820 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A card model checks whether panels close.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:28.220 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A grid checks location and direction.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:33.565 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tracing helps compare a shape with its translated, reflected
or rotated image.

00:01:33.565 --> 00:01:36.925 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose the six-square cross as a cube kiosk model.

00:01:36.925 --> 00:01:40.245 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
All six faces are equal squares, each fold joins a whole
matching edge, and the panels occupy different positions
when closed.

00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:44.445 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A successful paper fold supports the design.

00:01:44.445 --> 00:01:51.938 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A colourful drawing without a fold check would provide
weaker evidence.

00:01:51.938 --> 00:01:55.298 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The delivery depot starts at two, two on our unit grid.

00:01:55.298 --> 00:01:59.058 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Plot a dot at the intersection rather than in the middle of
a cell.

00:01:59.058 --> 00:02:01.218 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read across first and then up.

00:02:01.218 --> 00:02:08.402 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Giving the planner this starting pair makes the later route
reproducible instead of depending on a vague location
description.

00:02:08.402 --> 00:02:13.042 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The courier moves four units right, three units up and then
two units left.

00:02:13.042 --> 00:02:13.602 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the final coordinates.

00:02:13.602 --> 00:02:20.882 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Write an intermediate pair after each instruction so you can
check the route piece by piece.

00:02:20.882 --> 00:02:25.252 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the original depot point visible while tracking the
latest position.

00:02:30.252 --> 00:02:32.012 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four right gives six, two.

00:02:32.012 --> 00:02:33.772 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three up gives six, five.

00:02:33.772 --> 00:02:35.572 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two left gives four, five, the kiosk location.

00:02:35.572 --> 00:02:37.052 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The courier travelled nine units in total.

00:02:37.052 --> 00:02:45.972 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The final coordinates describe a position, while the sum of
the movement distances describes the route length.

00:02:45.972 --> 00:02:47.996 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both checks belong in the plan.

00:02:47.996 --> 00:02:52.476 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A separate triangular garden motif must slide three right
and two up.

00:02:52.476 --> 00:02:55.076 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every vertex follows the same movement.

00:02:55.076 --> 00:02:58.036 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For example, one, one becomes four, three.

00:02:58.036 --> 00:02:58.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check the other vertices too.

00:02:58.356 --> 00:03:07.102 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The garden shape keeps its side lengths, angles and
orientation, even though its location on the park map
changes.

00:03:07.102 --> 00:03:11.822 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A sketch can look almost correct while one vertex is a grid
unit out of place.

00:03:11.822 --> 00:03:15.022 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check numerical relationships against the drawing.

00:03:15.022 --> 00:03:22.862 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A net needs matching edges, a route needs correct coordinate
changes, and a transformation needs its own invariant rules.

00:03:22.862 --> 00:03:25.884 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Appearance alone cannot establish all three.

00:03:25.884 --> 00:03:28.084 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A sign point starts at two, three.

00:03:28.084 --> 00:03:30.124 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First slide it two units right.

00:03:30.124 --> 00:03:34.084 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then reflect its new position in the vertical mirror through
five.

00:03:34.084 --> 00:03:36.684 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These instructions describe a sequence.

00:03:36.684 --> 00:03:42.881 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The input to the reflection is the result of the slide, so
keep that intermediate point in the record.

00:03:42.881 --> 00:03:48.081 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start at two, three, slide two right, then reflect across
the vertical line through five.

00:03:48.081 --> 00:03:50.361 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the final coordinates.

00:03:50.361 --> 00:03:53.161 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which point is actually being reflected?

00:03:53.161 --> 00:03:58.770 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Measure its distance from the mirror after the slide, rather
than measuring from the original point.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.409 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The slide moves two, three to four, three.

00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:09.170 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That point lies one unit left of the mirror.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:12.650 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its reflection lies one unit right, at six, three.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.969 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The completed sequence therefore sends the original sign
point to six, three.

00:04:14.969 --> 00:04:20.957 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The intermediate pair explains how we obtained the answer.

00:04:20.957 --> 00:04:24.077 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Try reflecting the original two, three first.

00:04:24.077 --> 00:04:25.677 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It becomes eight, three.

00:04:25.677 --> 00:04:28.557 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Sliding that result two right gives ten, three.

00:04:28.557 --> 00:04:30.437 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This differs from six, three, so the two operations cannot
simply be exchanged.

00:04:30.437 --> 00:04:38.956 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The example shows why a design must communicate the order of
transformations clearly.

00:04:38.956 --> 00:04:42.636 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our kiosk cube belongs to the rectangular-prism family.

00:04:42.636 --> 00:04:47.956 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six faces alone would not identify it, because a long carton
has six faces too.

00:04:47.956 --> 00:04:51.996 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
All six cube faces are squares, and its edges have equal
lengths.

00:04:51.996 --> 00:04:56.211 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A classification statement should name the properties
supporting the label.

00:04:56.211 --> 00:04:59.131 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A useful review checks each claim separately.

00:04:59.131 --> 00:04:59.411 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does the kiosk close?

00:04:59.411 --> 00:05:03.451 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Are landmarks at the stated coordinates?

00:05:03.451 --> 00:05:06.771 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do route steps reach their labelled destinations?

00:05:06.771 --> 00:05:11.091 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does each transformation follow its named line, centre or
movement?

00:05:11.091 --> 00:05:16.481 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Separate checks help locate an error without discarding the
parts of the plan that already work.

00:05:16.481 --> 00:05:22.281 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Create a small park map with three landmarks, a checked
kiosk net and a transformed motif.

00:05:22.281 --> 00:05:28.001 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask a partner to reproduce one route and one transformation
from your written instructions.

00:05:28.001 --> 00:05:33.332 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What evidence would convince you that your plan is precise,
rather than merely attractive?

00:05:38.332 --> 00:05:42.012 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare your partner’s results with your intended map.

00:05:42.012 --> 00:05:45.852 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If something differs, find the first step where the evidence
separates.

00:05:45.852 --> 00:05:48.532 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Correct that instruction and test again.

00:05:48.532 --> 00:05:54.332 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Strong spatial reasoning connects models, coordinates and
properties, and explains why a result works.

00:05:54.332 --> 00:05:58.868 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It also treats a discovered error as useful information for
improvement.

00:05:58.868 --> 00:06:00.668 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:06:00.668 --> 00:06:04.548 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Combine checked nets, routes and transformations in one
design.

00:06:04.548 --> 00:06:09.588 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:06:09.588 --> 00:06:14.228 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:06:14.228 --> 00:06:20.771 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
